Kristiansen for their replies.

My question was:
I have a question concerning the optimization of smooth functions. I am 
using S+2k on Win NT. I would be glad to receive some hints.

Problem: find a x in argmin f(x) st. b <= x <= c , where f is in C^2 and a 
global minimum exists for vectors b < c.

nlminb is appropriate to find a global minimum if the function is 
quasi-convex (and things are running not too bad) otherwise one is only 
assured to find a local minimum.

Has someone implemented a global search algorithm, e.g. a Sobol-sequence, 
to approximate a global minimum or at least to find some reasonable 
starting points for nlminb?

Is someone aware of free code for Low Discrepancy Sequences (in C, FORTRAN, 
S+; to my knowledge there is code available on the ACM-page and from 
"Numerical Receipts in C", but not for free)?

Are there better solutions to this problem which found their way to S+?

With many thanx for your attention,
Thomas Siwik

And here are the answers by authors:

***** Tim Hesterberg *****

>Is someone aware of free code for Low Discrepancy Sequences (in C, FORTRAN,

>S+; to my knowledge there is code available on the ACM-page and from 
>"Numerical Receipts in C", but not for free)?

Below is code to generate Hammersley sequences.
However, I don't recommend these sequences except for the easiest
applications. 
I believe the Sobol sequences are better, but I don't have freely-available
code
for them.

I'd apprecate a summary of other responses.

hammersley <- function(m, b){
# Convert integer m to its Hammersley point mod b
# m may be a vector, e.g. 1:k for some k; all values must be positive # To 
generate d-dimensional low-discrepancy points, call this function # d times,

setting b in turn to each of the first d prime numbers.
n <- length(b)
result <- numeric(n)
factor <- 1/b
while(any(m)) {
result <- result + factor * (m %% b) m <- m %/% b
factor <- factor/b
}
result
}

