**** Tyson Hillard Holmes ****

#1

I put a related question to the newsgroup a little while back, but received
only
a few responses.

I have been working on a particularly difficult global optimization problem
and 
recently had success with genetic algorithms. These procedures permit great 
latitiude in design and are not difficult to code in S. In concept, one
treats 
the parameter vector as something akin to a chromosone. An initial
population of
these vectors (20 seems to be an optimal quantity) is generated and then 
subjected to stochastic recombination and mutation. Differential
"reproduction" 
for the "generation" of vectors is based on evaluation of the objective 
function. Convergence is fairly rapid (ca. 10 iterations). Lineages can
converge
on local optima; so I typically generate several lineages to obtain the
global 
optimum.

Hope this helps,

Tyson

