From jdavis at PDF.COM  Tue Sep 12 19:53:05 2000
From: jdavis at PDF.COM (Joseph C. Davis)
Date: Tue, 12 Sep 2000 14:53:05 -0500
Subject: [S] SUMMARY: Un-stacking a ragged array
Message-ID: <39BE89A1.318701D2@pdf.com>

Hello S-news:

There were quite a few responses to my request for hints on how to un-stack
an array with different numbers of entries for each of the levels of hte
'unstacking' variable. These responses fell generally into 4 categories:

1) use match to create a new array. This is the method that I implemented.
Please see (undocumented) code below. Many thanks to the respondents:
    David James <dj at research.bell-labs.com>
    Tim Hesterberg <timh at statsci.com>
    Hakon K. Gjessing" <h.k.gjessing at basalmed.uio.no

2) tapply. This one seems quite elegant, except that tapply can return a
multi-dimensional array and I really want is a 2-d array. This is another
issue that I've never quite taken the time to work on -- a generic function
to un-wrap a multi-dim array into a 2-d array. The 2-d array is much less
compact, but is suitable for many of the Splus functions. Another
alternative is to recode the plotting routines to work on n-dim arrays
instead of 2-d arrays. Anyway, I didn't implement this alg.
  Ed Kademan <kademan at phz.com>
  Julian Taylor <jtaylor at biometricssa.adelaide.edu.au
  Anne York <anne.york at noaa.gov>

tapply(dfr$Fails, dfr[c("wafer", "bin")], sum)

3) Use crosstabs. Nice application.:   Berwin Turlach
<berwin at maths.uwa.edu.au

> tt <- crosstabs(Fails~wafer+bin, data=jcd)
> tt1 <- matrix(as.vector(tt),nrow=nrow(tt))
> dimnames(tt1) <- dimnames(tt)
> tt1[tt1==0]<-NA
> tt1

4) Use reShape, from HMISC. The reShape function seems really powerful, but
I have unfortunately never figured out how to use it effectively. I'm
assuming user error here. :
Frank E Harrell Jr<fharrell at virginia.edu

Here is the original posting:

"Joseph C. Davis" wrote:

> Hi S-gurus,
>
> It is often the case that different data structures are more convenient
> for different types of anlaysis. Since I use Trellis a great deal, I
> tend to keep my data in 'stacked' format, so that I have a single
> response column and a set of columns identifying the 'design variables.'
>
> Sometimes, however, it is useful to make a 'wide' format, where you take
> the response vector and cut it up according to one or more of the design
> variables and create a new matrix with N columns, where N is the number
> of levels in the design variable which you used.
>
> If there are equal numbers of instances for all levels of the design
> variable, then this is very easy. I wrote a function to do this long ago
> and the PC version of Splus now has an un-stack function.
>
> However, if there are un-equal instances of for each level of the design
> variable, then I don't know an elegant solution for creating a complete
> array containing all instances and having either "NA's" or something
> else in the spots for missing data.
>
> For instance a typical case might be as follows:
>
> > wafer   bin     Fails
> > 1       29      6
> > 1       33      2
> > 1       54      4
> > 1       66      6
> > 2       29      98
> > 2       44      2
> > 2       54      6
> > 2       69      4
> >
>
> and I want to get something like the following:
>
> > wafer   bin29   bin33   bin44   bin54   bin66   bin69
> > 1       6       2       NA      4       6       NA
> > 2       98      NA      2       6       NA      4
> >
> Does someone out there have an elegant/fast solution for this?
>
> Thanks for any and all help!
>
> regards,
> jcd

