5            146     10     0     24/09/1997        0.07
3.21                  21.10
6            146     10     0     20/11/1997        0.09
3.07                  23.70

i.e. row 2 in the final dataframe is made up of rows 2 & 3 in the first
dataframe, empty rows 7 - 9 are removed, and the first response is taken
from rows 10 - 12 for Var1 and Var2, but the only reponse for Var3 which
is in row 11 is used.

The problem is that all of the methods I have seen so far on S-News
(!duplicated etc) retain the first row and simply remove the duplicate
rows which follow.  This does not work in this case, as some of the
information may be in the second or third row, and is likely to be in
different rows for different responses.

I have written some code to do this, but it uses 2 loops, is very memory
intensive and takes a very long time to run.  I have no idea how to
adapt the vectorised solutions which have been suggested in the past to
this situation (but would like to be able to use something as simple and
elegant!).

On a more general note - I frequently loop through the response columns
to produce graphs etc.  Is there a more efficient way to perform the
same operation on several columns? (tapply doesn't seem to work well in
this case (?) ).

Any suggestions appreciated...

