From Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au  Fri Sep 15 01:13:59 2000
From: Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Fri, 15 Sep 2000 11:13:59 +1000
Subject: Re: [S] categorical (factor) variable handling in S-PLUS and SAS
Message-ID: <3.0.32.20000915111357.01227aa0@pophost.nsw.cmis.csiro.au>

At 16:51 14/09/00 -0400, Gilliam, Reid wrote:
>
>Hi all,
>
>I am interested in evaluating the differences between logistic regression
>output in S-PLUS and SAS.  The situation is this: I have a model of the
>following form:
>
>Y ~ X*
>
>where X* is a categorical (factor) variable with 4 distinct values.

This is a perennial issue that attracts far too much prominence.  We
discuss it  in (probably too much) detail in MASS, V&R, (3rd Edition)
Section 6.2, pp154-161.  I won't repeat this lengthy discussion here, but
let me interpose a few comments that might be useful.

>
>I have used SAS and S-PLUS to run a logistic regression (SAS: PROC LOGISTIC,
>S-PLUS: glm(binomial family with logit function)).  The results are very
>similar in terms of model fit and regression coefficients when I manually
>create dummy variables based on the values of X* in each language (i.e., X1,
>X2, and X3).
>
>I would like to have SAS and S-PLUS create these dummy variables
>automatically without my having to create these manually.

Creating dummy variable matrices manually (if you are really convinced you
need to do it) is no big deal, actually.  Suppose Xf is a factor and you
want a complete set of dummy binary variables to represent it.  (Emphasize,
*complete*.)  Then all you need to do is

Xb <- model.matrix(~ Xf - 1)

and now Xb is an n x k matrix of binary variables that can be used directly
in a glm model specification, such as

Y ~ Xb

you will, of course, get one of the levels ditched since the full model
matrix has linearly dependent columns, but I guess you know that.

(The important side-point to note is that matrices can enter model formulae
to generate multiple columns of a model matrix.)

Rather than generate a *complete* set of binary variables for a factor what
you probably want to do is generate a *reduced* set, that is all but the
first so that the linear dependence referred to above is avoided.  All you
need to do for this to happen automatically is to set the default contrast
matrix to do so with

options(contrasts = rep("contr.treatment", 2))

Now ALL factors in linear model formulae will translate into (reduced)
binary variable representations in the generated model matrix.

>I know that S-PLUS creates these automatically provided that the variable is
>identified as a "factor" variable.  However, I don't think glm() creates
>simple 0/1 dummy variables when it runs the regression.  I've heard that it
>instead creates linear combinations of dummy variables as covariates in the
>logistic regression model.  Is this true?

In a sense it is true, but there are some misconceptions in what you say
above.

All model matrices for the standard modelling fitting functions, including
lm, glm, aov, and several others, are created by the function
model.matrix() referred to above.  So it is a question of what
model.matrix() does, not S-PLUS or even glm().

Briefly, if Xb is your *complete* binary matrix for a factor model.matrix()
generates columns of the form (Xb %*% C) where C is the "contrast matrix".
The precise form of the contrast matrix depends on several things,
including what you have set as your "contrasts" options (see above) and
what has gone before this term in the model itself.  The "contr.treatment"
options arranges for a C to be chosen that simply removes the first column
of Xb, thus leaving a reduced set of binary variables to enter the model
matrix.

When you get to composite terms such as A*B or A*B/C, of course, there is
an added layer of complication.

>The concern I have is that S-PLUS and SAS compute very different regression
>coefficients because they handle factor/categorical variables differently.

Of course.  Why I think this is a trivial issue, though, is because far too
much emphasis is given to regression *coefficients*.  Generally speaking
you should (probably) be much more concerned with the deviance, the fitted
model and predictions made from it, all of which are *invariant* with
respect to the choice of contrast matrix and hence should be the same for
S-PLUS and SAS up to numerical accuracy.

>For example:
>
>*********************************************************************
>In SAS, I ran the following logistic regression model using PROC LOGISTIC:
>
>Y ~ X1 + X2 + X3, where X1, X2, and X3 were hardcoded dummy variables.
>
>Here are the results:
>
>Variable               Parameter Estimate      Standard Error
>INTERCEPT      -1.5133                 0.1594
>X1             1.5144                  0.1628
>X2             1.3826                  0.1599
>X3             0.9510                  0.1621
>*********************************************************************
>
>*********************************************************************
>In S-PLUS, I ran the following logistic regression model using glm with the
>binomial family and logit function:
>
>Y ~ X*, where X* is the factor variable represented by hardcoded dummy
>variables X1, X2, and X3 above.
>
>Here are the results:
>
>Variable               Parameter Estimate      Standard Error
>INTERCEPT      -0.5474                 0.0388
>X1             -0.0659                 0.0176
>X2             -0.1658                 0.0113
>X3             -0.3168                 0.0373
>*********************************************************************

Whoa!  I do not believe you have generated the same binary variables in the
two systems.  This is clearly some mistake.  

>QUESTIONS:
>1.     Which one of these outputs is likely to be more appropriate and why?

My response would be that in glm's (or even lm's) the emphasis given to
regression coefficients is often misplaced and predictions are usually more
central to the real problem.  In this respect both should be identical
regardless of contrast matrices.

>2.     How would one take the parameter estimates in the S-PLUS output and
>use the logistic regression model equation to "score" new records?

If I understand you correctly, if glm.obj is the fitted model object, all
you need do is 

scores <- predict(glm.obj, new.records, type = "link")

and it simply deals with whatever form of coefficients you have.

>3.     Is there a way to have S-PLUS create the dummy variables
>automatically by creating strict 0/1 dummy variables for the values of the
>categorical variable?

Yes, using 

options(contrasts = c("contr.treatment", "contr.treatment"))

This is something that can be done quite subliminally in your .First function.

Note that this will usually create a reduced set of dummy variables where
the reduction is merely to allow for intrinsic aliasing.  The reduction is
done by omitting the *first* variable, by the way, so the remaining levels
become comparisons with the first.

Bill Venables.

