From Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au  Fri Sep 15 01:38:37 2000
From: Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Fri, 15 Sep 2000 11:38:37 +1000
Subject: Re: [S] Introducing S-PLUS 6
In-Reply-To: <AFD78192EC49D311BFAE00902798AB8F0BDA9F@jupiter.sc.arbitrade.com>
Message-ID: <3.0.32.20000915113836.01298860@pophost.nsw.cmis.csiro.au>

At 17:12 14/09/00 -0500, Vadim Ogranovich wrote:
>
>Hi, I just received a brochure from MathSoft introducing S-PLUS 6. One of
>the reasons for upgrade on Windows mentioned there was "Improved Performance
>and Large Data Set Analysis...", which would "allow to analyze arbitrary
>large data sets...". Interestingly this is not mentioned as a reason for
>upgrade on Unix. Does this mean that Unix already allows analysis of data
>sets that do not fit in RAM? 

You have to be very careful when you read this.  There is improved
performance on large data sets using the standard fitting functions but not
as much as everyone wants.  There is also increased flexibility with input
and output, in particular you can now read a file in sections leaving the
file open between reads.  This (as far as I can see) is what "allows" *you*
to analyze arbitrarily large data sets - you can now handle them in pieces.
 However it is not true that you can do it with the standard modelling
tools, by and large *you* have to do it yourself from scratch using a bit
of programming and fairly low-level tools.  There is some demo software
around that does automate the process for you with very simple kinds of
linear model, but that's about it as far as I can see.  Of course in the
future the challenge will be to produce forms of the fitting functions that
will allow you to handle arbitrarily large data sets in this way, but you
will need to have several passes over the data and it could (for a while at
least) be pretty slow, particularly with things like glm where the process
is iterative.

>Are there any benchmark results that would
>allow me to see if the new S-PLUS is capable of handling problems of the
>sizes I am dealing with? For example some benchmarks for "plain" linear
>regression would already be helpful.

That's something I will leave for MathSoft to ponder.  (Of course as a
complete programming language S, and hence S-PLUS, is in principle capable
of anything but I don't imagine that is the sort of answer you are looking
for!)

There are some time comparisons between S-PLUS 5.1 and S-PLUS 3.4 on the
same machine in "S Programming", but I doubt this is the kind of thing you
are looking for here and even now they are probably a bit out of date.

Bill Venables.
